1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 With cost-effectiveness analysis we already included one non-monetary criterion in our analysis.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 But what if we want to include more extra criteria?

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
 Well, then we have to go to multi-criteria decision making.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 In fact, multi-criteria decision making has two main streams, I would say.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
 There's multi-objective decision making, which is more the design type of decision making,

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 where there's an infinite large number of choices and we want to find the best option using a simultaneous optimization of competing criteria.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 Typical concept there is a Pareto front.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 Multi-attribute decision making, that's what we will do here, is more about selecting, evaluating,

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 and there we work with a discrete, limited number of predetermined alternatives.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
 The alternatives we try to prioritize to rank which one we want to choose the best.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 Note that in practice often multi-criteria decision making is used as a synonym for the last, for the multi-attribute decision making.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 So here you see an example of multi-objective decision making, but this is out of scope here.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 So here we have two examples with multiple criteria.

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 The first is about three investment projects for enhancing occupational safety and working conditions beyond what is legally required.

15
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 And then the question is which one is the best.

16
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 We only have one cost per project, but we have two effects, two criteria that measure the quality of the projects

17
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 and it's not quite clear which one is the best.

18
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 We can't do an incremental analysis as we did before because there are two effects and we didn't see any technique yet to cope with that problem.

19
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
 Another example found in quite a few handbooks is about the hospital service in Brooklyn.

20
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 They consider investment in a reorganization.

21
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 They have some ideas. They can keep the number of ambulances, which is currently seven.

22
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 So we can keep that at seven, but relocate them to a satellite garage.

23
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 They could increase the number of ambulances to ten and relocate them in a satellite garage.

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 Or they could have a number of cruising ambulances, six, eight or ten, instead of ambulances stationed at a garage.

25
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 And then how to choose between these alternative scenarios.

26
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 Well, the costs are rather simple to estimate, straightforward, but the impacts to consider.

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 Well, how will you measure the effect?

28
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
 And then you could say, of course, well, the quality of health care.

29
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 I could measure the number of deaths avoided by reorganizing the service.

30
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 But then, of course, it's relevant, but it's not practical.

31
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 Remember that practical was also one of the criteria for our effectiveness measure.

32
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 Because why it's not practical?

33
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 Well, first of all, hospitals will not be willing to share that information because of patient privacy.

34
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 And moreover, it's difficult to estimate whether or not being five minutes earlier at the hospital would have saved a patient's life or not.

35
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 Maybe in some cases it's possible, but certainly not in all cases.

36
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
 So we have to find some other way.

37
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 And a way of doing it is, for instance, by saying, well, we could look at the average time for an ambulance to pick up a patient.

38
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 That's one measure.

39
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
 But of course, we do not want the average time to be OK.

40
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
 But with, for instance, a normal distribution with a very long tail, that's saying, well, average time is OK.

41
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 But there are patients that are never picked up or only picked up after hours.

42
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 So we will also have a measure, which is the number of quick interventions.

43
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 So less than 20 minutes.

44
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 How we evaluate that, because it's difficult?

45
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 Well, we will build a simulation model for that.

46
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
 And here you see what is happening with the process, in fact.

47
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 We have to picture the process.

48
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 So there's some waiting time.

49
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,000
 There's a dispatch delay.

50
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 Then the ambulance is traveling to the patient.

51
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
 There's a pickup delay.

52
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
 Then traveling back to the hospital, a drop off and then back to the base.

53
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,000
 But in fact, we only have to take into account the dark blue ones, because these are the things that are different between the scenarios that we want to investigate.

54
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 Picking up a patient, well, once the ambulance is there, it doesn't matter where or how it came there.

55
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
 It's just the same time for all cases.

56
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
 OK, so that's giving you some idea of what happens when you pick up a patient.

57
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
 And then you can see that the ambulance is there.

58
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
 It's just the same time for all cases.

59
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 OK, so that's giving you some idea on the complex.

60
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 OK, so let's have a closer look at the multi attribute decision making methods that are around.

61
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 Often, as I told you, the MCDM is used as a synonym.

62
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
 Now, just copied the list from Wikipedia.

63
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 So you see that there are quite a number of different methods available.

64
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
 So picking the best method is not straightforward, because all methods have strong and weak points.

65
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:54,000
 Just for illustrative purposes, you have a table there pointing out what criteria you could use for picking a method.

66
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
 Is literature helpful?

67
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:05,000
 Well, there are many different classification frameworks for multi criteria decision making methods.

68
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 So there's a lot around, but there's no straight answer to what is the best method.

69
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:24,000
 One way of classifying MCDM methods is by making a difference between non compensatory ones and compensatory ones.

70
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
 The non compensatory ones do not permit trade-offs between attributes.

71
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 So if you have an unfavorable value in one attribute, it cannot be offset by a favorable one in another attribute.

72
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
 Each attribute stands on its own.

73
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 Typical examples, as you see there, are the maxmin and the maxmax.

74
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 These methods, however, are only applicable when attribute values are comparable with one another,

75
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:54,000
 because they are measured in the same unit or transformed to a common scale.

76
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:02,000
 In the business context that we are looking at for engineering economy purposes, these are not used a lot.

77
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 Then we are looking at compensatory ones.

78
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 So a slight decline in one attribute is acceptable if it's compensated by an enhancement in one or more other attributes.

79
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:23,000
 We have three big classes, the value based methods that are using the value or the perception of the decision maker in real numbers.

80
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:32,000
 Outranking methods, where we use a principle of dominance, however, without attaching a numerical value to that preference.

81
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:43,000
 And then the reference point methods, also called compromise ranking, which are based on a distance to an ideal situation or a distance away from a worst case.

82
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,000
 We will see examples of the value based ones and the reference point ones.

83
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,000
 So the methods that are underlined, we will see an example.

84
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
 But first, what are the steps?

85
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:05,000
 Well, the steps are that we start with alternatives that are well defined, discrete and mutually exclusive and preferably a limited number.

86
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:16,000
 So these methods are not fit to compare a hundred different alternatives, so we rather stick to a few alternatives.

87
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
 Then we have to decide on criteria.

88
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:22,000
 We have to assign weights to the criteria.

89
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:27,000
 We have to assign scores for the alternatives for each of the criteria.

90
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:34,000
 Then somehow we have to aggregate the information and then we have to make an evaluation and selection.

91
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 OK, this is just also for illustrative purposes.

92
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,000
 This is an example of the use of MCDA in health care.

93
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
 OK, let's start with the criteria.

94
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
 Picking criteria is not straightforward.

95
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:01,000
 We have to be careful that we find good criteria, so we should involve all stakeholders in the decision process.

96
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
 We should have enough, but not too many criteria.

97
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
 So rule of thumb is that the number of criteria is between five and ten.

98
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:21,000
 If you have more criteria, it becomes very not very difficult, but it becomes a very tedious job to continuity analysis.

99
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
 There are some requirements and guidelines.

100
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:34,000
 Definitely exhaustive means that while we try to have enough criteria to cover all important aspects.

101
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
 Non-redundant, we don't want to measure the same thing twice.

102
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
 Coherent, measurable, verifiable.

103
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
 So we want something that is clearly defined.

104
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:52,000
 For instance, if you say the number of accidents, that's easy to take into account.

105
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:00,000
 If you say flexibility, for instance, that's a lot more difficult to define and to measure.

106
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
 An example.

107
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:09,000
 So the setting is the manufacturer of industrial vehicles, the painting department.

108
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:17,000
 The problem at hand is they want to buy new masks because of occupational safety,

109
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
 occupational diseases, because you're working with solvents if you paint.

110
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:31,000
 Technical risks, legislation and a market study left the company with three alternatives.

111
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 Now they have to select between those three alternatives.

112
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
 So three, the alternative each come from a different supplier.

113
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,000
 And this list that you see there is what they think to take into account.

114
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
 I would suggest that you have a look at it.

115
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
 Think about these criteria.

116
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:55,000
 Think about how would you handle that to take the analysis one step further.

117
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
 I will not discuss it now.

118
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:04,000
 I suggest that we discuss it in the open office session that is coming up.

119
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,000
 OK, then we have to decide on the weights, the scores.

120
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
 Two types.

121
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
 We have the compositional ones and the decompositional methods.

122
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:20,000
 In compositional methods, and that's the ones that we will be looking at here,

123
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:25,000
 criteria weights and alternative scores are evaluated separately.

124
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:32,000
 So first, as you see, the decision maker decides on the relative importance of the criteria by setting weights.

125
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,000
 Then the decision maker assigns scores for each criteria for all alternatives.

126
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
 And then one and two are combined.

127
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
 We will see examples of that in a minute.

128
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:54,000
 The decompositional methods, they are like discrete choice experiments, paprika as an example.

129
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:01,000
 And you see from the example there, the decision maker is presented with a choice between two situations.

130
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:11,000
 And in these two situations, already an evaluation of the criteria is given.

131
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:16,000
 The example comes from a cobot selection problem.

132
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:21,000
 Now, how to assign weights to the criteria?

133
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
 Well, there are different methods.

134
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
 Here you see classification from literature.

135
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,000
 So we have subjective methods, objective methods, integrated methods.

136
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:35,000
 In the remainder of this chapter, we will focus on subjective weighting methods.

137
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:40,000
 And as a name is telling you, these are based on expert opinion.

138
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
 There's a direct method.

139
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:55,000
 So the basic option is that you allocate a budget of 100 points as a decision maker over the different criteria.

140
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:02,000
 And the more important criteria is, the more points are allocated and thus the higher the weight is.

141
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,000
 You see an example there in the table.

142
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:11,000
 There are many, many, many modifications possible.

143
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:22,000
 One modification is that you say to the decision maker, give a weight of 100 for the criteria that you find the most important.

144
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 And then assign values to the other criteria that are...

145
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:44,000
 Instead of asking directly to the decision maker to assign weights, we can use an indirect ranking,

146
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:51,000
 where you're asking the decision maker to rank the criteria in order of decreasing importance for him or her.

147
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
 And then we use a formula to determine the weights.

148
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
 Different formulas are possible.

149
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:00,000
 As you see, there's a rank sum, the rank exponential, the rank reciprocal.

150
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:05,000
 And in the table below, you see the outcome.

151
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:09,000
 We can choose between six study subjects.

152
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:18,000
 We see the ranking in the second column and then we see the weights that are assigned in each of the cases and also normalized.

153
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
 So they can be quite different.

154
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:25,000
 Maybe this example to go a little bit deeper on the difference.

155
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:29,000
 So we have an example where we want to buy a car.

156
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:35,000
 There are seven criteria that we can take into account and we rank them from one to seven.

157
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:41,000
 So we are most concerned about safety while the brand is not so important to us.

158
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:46,000
 The table shows you that in case we would have a uniform weight,

159
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:53,000
 then the weights would be 0.1429.

160
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:57,000
 If we use the rank sum formula, we have these weights.

161
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
 If you use the rank reciprocal, we have those weights.

162
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:06,000
 Now, I want to draw your attention to the fact that there's a big difference,

163
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:13,000
 especially for the first criteria, at least the criteria ranked first.

164
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:21,000
 If you look here, you see that in case we use a rank sum, there's not so much difference.

165
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:28,000
 Well, of course, the weights are decreasing, but not by big steps.

166
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:34,000
 While here, if you look at this, at the rank reciprocal,

167
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:40,000
 we see that the first criterion gets a very high weight and then the weights are decreasing.

168
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,000
 So it depends a bit on the decision-maker's attitude or preference.

169
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,000
 If the decision-maker really feels very strong about, in this case, safety

170
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:57,000
 and really wants to emphasize that by putting a very high weight on the safety,

171
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,000
 then we go for this approach.

172
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:05,000
 If there is a ranking by the decision-maker,

173
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:10,000
 but the decision-maker is not really very keen on having very high weights

174
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
 on the first we ranked criterion, we go for that option.

175
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:23,000
 So we can, in fact, fine-tune the weights by choosing a method

176
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:29,000
 that is adopted to our decision-making attitude.

177
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:34,000
 Pairwise comparison is an often used method.

178
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
 The decision-maker is comparing the criteria two by two,

179
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
 so we need quite some comparisons.

180
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:44,000
 That's one of the reasons why it's important not to have too many criteria.

181
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:48,000
 The idea is that it's easy for the decision-maker to compare two by two,

182
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:54,000
 that making a ranking from a list of ten is more difficult.

183
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,000
 So the decision-maker is comparing them two by two

184
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:03,000
 and is expressing the perception of the relative importance of the criteria

185
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:11,000
 by an intensity value, so I find A five times more important than B, for instance.

186
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:15,000
 From there, the criterion weights are calculated.

187
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:21,000
 A nice touch in this method is that we also can do a check on the consistency

188
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,000
 of the decision-maker by computing a consistency ratio,

189
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
 and if it's not okay, we can take further action.

190
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
 Decision-makers are sometimes not consistent.

191
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:36,000
 They say that they find A more important than B, B more important than C,

192
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:41,000
 but then they say that C is more important than A, which is a little bit strange.

193
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
 So that's a nice touch of this method.

194
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:50,000
 Example for an investment in COTS for a hospital.

195
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:54,000
 There are three criteria, so it's just a little example.

196
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:59,000
 It's about adjustment range, adjustment easiness of these side panels,

197
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:03,000
 and also patient safety is considered.

198
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:08,000
 So we are saying that adjustment range is more important than adjustment easiness,

199
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,000
 and it's even three times more important.

200
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:16,000
 Adjustment range is less important than patient safety, intensity factor five.

201
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:23,000
 Adjustment easiness is less important than patient safety with an intensity factor of seven.

202
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
 So we put that in a matrix.

203
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:29,000
 Obviously here we have one, and if we have a three here, we have one over three there.

204
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
 If we have a five there, we have one over five, et cetera.

205
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:39,000
 Definitely if one is three times more important, then the other one is only one-third of importance.

206
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:45,000
 And then we do a normalization step, and we can compute our weights.

207
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
 Let's forget about the swing weighting.

208
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:57,000
 We already saw some methods, and this one is very complex.

209
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:02,000
 Another way of working is using brainstorming.

210
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
 So you just put all the stakeholders together around the table,

211
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:10,000
 and you start discussing until you have consensus on the weights that you want to use.

212
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:18,000
 In case the discussion goes out of hand, because there are some dominant figures

213
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:22,000
 and some people that really want to push through their opinion,

214
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:30,000
 you can go for a nominal group technique, which is a more structured way of doing a brainstorm,

215
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:38,000
 which also ensures a more fair input for all groups.

216
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:43,000
 Okay, the next step that we need to take is we need to aggregate the information

217
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:47,000
 and to select and evaluate the alternatives.

218
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:50,000
 So we will see some examples.

219
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:59,000
 We will see the linear additive methods, sometimes also called the simple additive weighting.

220
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:03,000
 It's a value-based method. It's easy. It's intuitive. It's often used.

221
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:09,000
 It has a drawback that it's rather simplistic, but still it can help decision-making.

222
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:20,000
 Then we will see an example of AHP-ANP, used a lot in companies, in the public sector, in healthcare,

223
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
 so a very popular one.

224
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:27,000
 And then we will see one example of the reference point method, namely the consensus method.

225
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
 It's just a basic example.

226
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:35,000
 And we will also illustrate with this example the concept of utility.

227
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:43,000
 So let's start with the linear additive method, sometimes also called simple additive weighting method.

228
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:51,000
 You see it's rather simple. We have our criteria. We have our weights. The sum of weights is 1.

229
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:59,000
 We have our alternatives. And each alternative is scored for each criterion,

230
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:05,000
 and then we make a weighted sum, weight times score plus weight times score, etc.

231
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:13,000
 So we end up with having a weighted score, weighted total score for each alternative and the one with the best wins.

232
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:21,000
 So it's rather simple, but still it can help our decision-making because it's making the decision more transparent.

233
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:26,000
 It's clear for everybody involved what were the scores. It's also clear what were the weights.

234
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:30,000
 So it's simple, but helpful.

235
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:39,000
 This is an example. So we have an automotive plant and they investigated four options for a production line.

236
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:43,000
 And besides money, so the present word,

237
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:54,000
 they also wanted to include aspects like speed, capacity, reliability, safety, flexibility, standardization.

238
00:21:54,000 --> 00:22:05,000
 They decided on weights. Every installation had a score for every one of the criteria.

239
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:17,000
 And then we make a weighted total score.

240
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:22,000
 This weighted total score, by the way, we could call a total effectiveness score.

241
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:31,000
 What this example illustrates is that it's in fact kind of a cost-effectiveness analysis.

242
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:44,000
 Point one about that is that effectiveness is here considered to be a composite measure.

243
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:47,000
 So we have different aspects of what we call effectiveness.

244
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:57,000
 While in the cost-effectiveness method we saw before, we only had one effectiveness measure, single one.

245
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:05,000
 In terms of multi-criteria decision-making, you could say that here we took out the monetary aspect.

246
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:14,000
 We could have considered a list with speed, reliability, safety, flexibility, standardization and cost.

247
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:20,000
 Here it was chosen not to do so, so cost was taken out.

248
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:32,000
 Sometimes this is done because people do not feel comfortable with cost being one of the criteria because costs, budgets, it's an important issue.

249
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:37,000
 And they like to see it as a separate element in the analysis.

250
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:40,000
 Let's have a look how we want to visualize things.

251
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:48,000
 Multi-criteria decision-making as any business tool is helping us to make decisions.

252
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
 It's not making decisions for us.

253
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:57,000
 So we want to discuss the outcome of multi-criteria decision-making analysis.

254
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
 We can work with the table.

255
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:07,000
 We could also go for a graphical representation where we visualize different scores.

256
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:11,000
 Each option has different scores, so we want to visualize that.

257
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:19,000
 Here in the bar chart we could also go for a radar plot, which is another representation.

258
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:27,000
 Personally, I think the radar plot is quite useful here because we see dominance.

259
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:42,000
 For instance, here we see that the yellow one is the option 4, is quite dominant for these three criteria, but it's dominated for the other.

260
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:48,000
 So it's providing some insight, but I must say that's a rather subjective evaluation.

261
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:55,000
 Some people might be more comfortable with the absolute scores in bar chart.

262
00:24:55,000 --> 00:25:04,000
 Also something that we could do, of course, is we could have cost, cost-effectiveness, cost versus effectiveness, and then we have different projects.

263
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:14,000
 And then we could see here the effectiveness border and points on the effectiveness border indicated for a given budget.

264
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:27,000
 No project with larger effectiveness could be found or that to have that effectiveness, this is the best alternative.

265
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:33,000
 The best alternative since it has the lowest cost for that level of effectiveness.

266
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:42,000
 The other method that we want to discuss is AHP and ANP.

267
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:45,000
 They were invented by Satti.

268
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
 Here you see how they work.

269
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:51,000
 So we have a goal, we have criteria, and there are alternatives.

270
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,000
 Now, why is it called hierarchy?

271
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:01,000
 Well, it's called hierarchy, as you see here, because we have different levels of criteria.

272
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:04,000
 We have here, it's a problem for supplier selection.

273
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:11,000
 We have product development capability, manufacturing capability, quality capability, and cost and time.

274
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:12,000
 So that's level one.

275
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:15,000
 We go one level in the hierarchy down.

276
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:23,000
 And then for each of these criteria is further split up in sub-criteria.

277
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:28,000
 You can imagine even more and more levels.

278
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:31,000
 So that's why it's called criteria.

279
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
 It's called hierarchy.

280
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:49,000
 So, if there is a link between the sub-criteria, we have to go for analytic network process,

281
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:52,000
 and then it becomes a little bit more complex.

282
00:26:52,000 --> 00:27:01,000
 So usually people try to formulate a problem so that we can just use a simple AHP.

283
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:06,000
 This is an example from a thesis.

284
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:16,000
 It was about maintenance work order, sorry, evaluation for fighting aircrafts.

285
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:23,000
 Not really the topic of this course, but what I want to show you here is the hierarchy.

286
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:27,000
 So we have, it's about the functional and technical aspects,

287
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:33,000
 and we want to consider maintenance quality, availability, and productivity and mission impact.

288
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:37,000
 If we see the weights here, the sum of weights is 100.

289
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:42,000
 For instance, availability has component reliability, maintainability and support,

290
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:47,000
 and also here the sum of weights is 100.

291
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:53,000
 In this case, this was used to evaluate maintenance work orders.

292
00:27:53,000 --> 00:28:02,000
 Another example of, and here we used ANP, was from a recent PhD,

293
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:08,000
 where the inventory performance management of a hospital was looked at.

294
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:14,000
 And here you see the different criteria that were important,

295
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:22,000
 and here you see the different stakeholders like pharmacy, operating theater, interventional radiology,

296
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:27,000
 and cardiovascular center, head of logistics department, and then we have an overall ranking.

297
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:30,000
 So you see different stakeholders can have different opinions,

298
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:36,000
 but how to tackle that is beyond the scope here.

299
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:41,000
 Now what is the consensus method? It's a reference point method.

300
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:50,000
 Basic idea here is that, well, this is an example here for two criteria.

301
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:55,000
 So we have U1 and U2.

302
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:00,000
 They are all utilities, but let's say they are criteria.

303
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:03,000
 I will explain the difference in a minute.

304
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:11,000
 So when they can vary between plus one and minus one, minus one and plus one is the score for the criteria.

305
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:17,000
 A definitely is the ideal situation because we have the maximum score for each of the criteria.

306
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:25,000
 Criteria B definitely is the worst case because we have the worst possible score for each of the two criteria.

307
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:32,000
 What we then have is, in fact, we have a plane in which we can locate our alternatives,

308
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:39,000
 and then we can pick the alternative, which is either the closest to the ideal situation

309
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:45,000
 or as far away as possible from the worst case.

310
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:54,000
 There is a slight difference, or there might be a slight difference in outcome as the one minute.

311
00:29:54,000 --> 00:30:00,000
 So if you want to be close here, you want to emphasize on the strong points of your alternatives.

312
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:14,000
 If you want to stay away from the worst point, you are definitely aiming at alternatives with no weak points or as few weak points as possible.

313
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:23,000
 So you express that with distance functions, and you can, by choosing your P value,

314
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:33,000
 you can have a Euclidean distance-like thing, or you can change the weight.

315
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:44,000
 Now, the utility function, it's offering you a possibility to, again, put in your own decision-maker attitude.

316
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:57,000
 Suppose that the performance scores for the criteria or the alternatives, let's say the alternatives, or between 0 and 10.

317
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:06,000
 Well, that's fine. That's what we asked the decision-maker. Please score the alternatives on a scale from 0 to 10.

318
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:14,000
 What is a little bit annoying is that for the method we need utilities, utilities between minus 1 and plus 1.

319
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:21,000
 So we have to convert our scale from 0 to 10 to a scale from minus 1 to plus 1.

320
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:30,000
 What we could do is just have a linear relationship. That makes a lot of sense, for instance, for costs.

321
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:37,000
 What we also could do is have another way. This is one which is used a lot.

322
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:48,000
 What does it mean? It means that, well, here I don't have a lot to say between a score of 0 or 1. It doesn't make a lot of difference for me.

323
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:54,000
 And here, well, 9 or 10, I don't see a lot of difference, but in between I want to make a distinction.

324
00:31:54,000 --> 00:32:05,000
 Or you could even say, well, you know, everything that is below 4, I don't care about. Everything that is above 5.5, I really like.

325
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:10,000
 Then you have this. So you can put your decision-maker's attitude in there.

326
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:22,000
 I already mentioned a few times the decision-maker's attitude that you could put in there.

327
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:34,000
 Now, is this positive? Well, yes, it's positive because decision-makers have their own perception, they have their own point of view, they have their own way of making decisions.

328
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:48,000
 So it's important, it's nice that you can put in your own attitude, but sometimes this is seen as a weakness of these methods because people can say that you can manipulate.

329
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:57,000
 Saying if you're not happy with the outcome, you just will change your utility function until you are having the outcome that you prefer.

330
00:32:57,000 --> 00:33:05,000
 An example from a glass-maker's company. So they're making flat glass for windows.

331
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:14,000
 So here is the production department and the window sheets. They are put on pallets, special pallets.

332
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:21,000
 And here the pallets are put on a transfer line. That's an input-output station.

333
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:33,000
 An AGV is picking up the pallets and is putting them in a warehouse, which is much bigger than a drone here, but okay, just for illustrational sake.

334
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:52,000
 If a customer is asking for windows, the sheets are, sorry, by the AGV is picking up the sheets from the warehouse, is putting them here, input-output station, and a transfer line is bringing them to the finishing department.

335
00:33:52,000 --> 00:34:06,000
 Now what is the problem? The problem is that this AGV sometimes has difficulties, it fails, it's getting stuck, and then somebody has to enter this area in order to fix it.

336
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:16,000
 That's rather dangerous, glass is a sharp thing, and if this AGV all of a sudden starts moving, occupational accidents can happen.

337
00:34:16,000 --> 00:34:33,000
 So the company wants to do something about it and they have some ideas. They can either put up posters, they can put the power-interruptor manual, so that's situated here, and before the operator is entering, he or she pushes.

338
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:46,000
 Or we can have a power-interruptor, which is an automated one, that if somebody is entering here, then the line stops and it can be done by the supplier or it can be done by a consultant.

339
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:59,000
 And then you have criteria that you want to take into account. Initial cost, maintenance cost, downtime at installation, mean time to repair, mean time between failure, worker satisfaction, impact on safety.

340
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:13,000
 I'll give you this example to show you that for these criteria we can have different units of measurement, we can even have a mix of qualitative and quantitative data, so here we have numbers.

341
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:25,000
 Here we have safety impact, very slight impact, considerable, considerable, same for worker satisfaction, so it's perfectly okay to have that as a starting situation.

342
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:33,000
 Of course we will have to convert that into numbers and these numbers will then be converted into utilities.

343
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:54,000
 So here you see the example, we have our alternatives, we have our numbers, we have the weights, we have the utility function, which here is a Gaussian utility function, so the one that is in the middle.

344
00:35:55,000 --> 00:36:02,000
 It was looking like this from the score from 0 to 10, minus 1 to plus 1.

345
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:12,000
 And here you see the difference with the distance functions and you can pick the one with the best outcome.

346
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:24,000
 So that's how it's working. Consensus method is a very simple example, you can do it in Excel, Vcore, Anthopsys, or also examples of this type of reference point method, but they are a little bit more complex.

347
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:34,000
 So a final note on MCDA is that it's popular and still increasing interest.

348
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:49,000
 What is now the benefit for the decision process? It's giving you a complete picture, it's not just about money, but you can include other criteria that can be tangible or less tangible.

349
00:36:49,000 --> 00:37:03,000
 It brings transparency to the decision making. I mentioned a few times as the stakeholders well that makes sure that the final solution will be accepted.

350
00:37:03,000 --> 00:37:12,000
 It's making it more easy to accept the decision if you have been part of the decision making instead of just being informed of the decision.

351
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:30,000
 You can incorporate your decision attitude, but of course picking the most appropriate methods can be quite hard because there are a lot of them around and not all decision makers have the time to spend reading about these methods in order to pick one.

352
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:49,000
 Data collection can be difficult, can take up a lot of time in case that stakeholders do not have the same opinion, that they stuck each with their own initial ideas, you have a problem and we have to be careful that we are not manipulated.

353
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:59,000
 In terms of tools, you might be quite happy to hear that there is a lot of software available, even open source software.

354
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:28,000
 Extra illustrations, which I will not discuss now, we have a separate session for that. This is an illustration of a paper, it's quite an old paper, but it's a nice illustration of a decision making situation, practical one where different elements come together and where you feel that at the end that a multi-criteria decision making tool would have been helpful in order to make a decision.

355
00:38:28,000 --> 00:38:41,000
 This is an example of the PhD I was telling about where A&P is used to select performance indicators for operating room supply chain management.

356
00:38:41,000 --> 00:39:10,000
 What has been done by Karen was that she made a long list of all possible performance indicators that could be used for that supply chain performance measurement and then what she did was using A&P to pick the ones that were important in a specific case, therefore she used the input of many of the stakeholders.

357
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:22,000
 This is a discussion case, this is also something I would like to briefly do with you in the open office if there is interest for that.

358
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:42,000
 It's a case that is illustrating or giving a flavor, I would not say illustrating because we do not have a lot of time to spend on it, but it's giving an indication, it's making you aware of how difficult it is or how easy it is to do a multi-criteria analysis in practice.

359
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:58,000
 Even if you stick with a very basic one like the linear additive method you can still run into a number of difficulties.

360
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:19,000
 The case is based on a real life case, so we have a medium sized company that is making light bulbs, there was a recent inspection whether or not governmental regulations were followed and some potential problems concerning occupational safety were found.

361
00:40:19,000 --> 00:40:28,000
 The threat for the company is that they have to pay fines or that they can even lose a license to operate if they do not quickly fix the problem.

362
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:48,000
 Then you see some alternatives that are there and in the next slide you see how we can solve it, but it would be nice if you could actually do the exercise to give an idea on the difficulties that you run into when you want to apply it in a real life situation.

363
00:40:48,000 --> 00:41:07,000
 So that was it for this chapter, we encountered the public sector and we saw the opportunity to take into account also non-monetary criteria in our analysis.

